From 42145dcf8bdeb94d08d4fd1f7e2a036715cac2ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leo <leoschlosser@tutamail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:51:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 88/89] fs: don't move a still-shared init fs_struct to nullfs

Since commit 9a8e29695888 ("fs: make userspace_init_fs a
dynamically-initialized pointer"), validate_fs_switch() points root and
pwd of userspace_init_fs at init_fs's root as soon as PID 1's
thread-group leader stops using it, i.e. on unshare(CLONE_FS) or
unshare(CLONE_NEWNS). Since commit 32750c77e811 ("fs: start all kthreads
in nullfs") that root is a private nullfs instance.

But userspace_init_fs is PID 1's own fs_struct. Every thread of PID 1
(pthreads are created with CLONE_FS) and every CLONE_FS child shares it,
and those tasks keep running on it after the leader switched away.
set_fs_root()/set_fs_pwd() pull the root out from under them, and every
path lookup they do afterwards fails with -ENOENT.

Android's init hits this on every boot. Its property service runs in a
separate thread, and SetupMountNamespaces() calls unshare(CLONE_NEWNS)
from the main thread. From then on, the property service thread can no
longer open /sys/fs/selinux/access. libselinux's avc_has_perm() fails
and reports every property_service:set check as an SELinux denial
(permissive=0, even with SELinux permissive), so init can't set any
property and boot does not complete:

  VFS: Pid 1 stopped sharing filesystem state
  audit: type=1107 ... msg='avc:  denied  { set } for
    property=ro.init.mnt_ns.count ... scontext=u:r:init:s0
    tcontext=u:object_r:init_mnt_ns_prop:s0 tclass=property_service
    permissive=0'

Only move the fs_struct to nullfs once nothing but the global
userspace_init_fs reference is left on it. While other tasks still use
it, it is live userspace state, not stale state, so leave it alone as
kernels before 7.3 did. A usermodehelper never shares it: copy_fs()
rejects CLONE_FS for COPY_FS_UMH and gives it a copy instead.

Fixes: 9a8e29695888 ("fs: make userspace_init_fs a dynamically-initialized pointer")

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 fs/fs_struct.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/fs/fs_struct.c b/fs/fs_struct.c
index 34699f3b6..106e36296 100644
--- a/fs/fs_struct.c
+++ b/fs/fs_struct.c
@@ -185,6 +185,8 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(unshare_fs_struct);
  */
 static inline void validate_fs_switch(struct fs_struct *old_fs)
 {
+	bool shared;
+
 	might_sleep();
 
 	if (likely(current->pid != 1))
@@ -192,7 +194,26 @@ static inline void validate_fs_switch(struct fs_struct *old_fs)
 	/* @old_fs may be dangling but for comparison it's fine */
 	if (old_fs != userspace_init_fs)
 		return;
-	pr_warn("VFS: Pid 1 stopped sharing filesystem state\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * userspace_init_fs holds its own reference, so @old_fs is still
+	 * alive here. Only PID 1's thread-group leader switched away: its
+	 * other threads (or CLONE_FS children) may still run on this
+	 * fs_struct. Android's init does exactly that: its property service
+	 * thread keeps using it after the main thread's unshare(CLONE_NEWNS).
+	 * Moving it to nullfs would make every path lookup of those tasks
+	 * fail with -ENOENT, so only do that once nothing but the global
+	 * reference is left.
+	 */
+	read_seqlock_excl(&old_fs->seq);
+	shared = old_fs->users > 1;
+	read_sequnlock_excl(&old_fs->seq);
+
+	pr_warn("VFS: Pid 1 stopped sharing filesystem state%s\n",
+		shared ? " (still used by other tasks, left alone)" : "");
+	if (shared)
+		return;
+
 	set_fs_root(userspace_init_fs, &init_fs.root);
 	set_fs_pwd(userspace_init_fs, &init_fs.root);
 }
